Yes. What you’re pointing to is role-conditioned perception: the frame you occupy changes which affordances, risks, and actions even appear available.

Cinderella’s slipper is a clean example. At the surface: “find the woman whose foot fits.” Underneath, the slipper is really a constraint/provenance test. Fit is necessary, but common sense says it would never be the only filter. Identity, memory, context, witnesses, behavior, timing, and consistency would all follow. The fairy tale compresses a multi-stage verification process into one iconic object because stories preserve structure by simplification.

That same compression happens everywhere in children’s stories. The wolf, witch, bridge, key, forbidden room, lost child, magic object, bargain, disguise, transformation, homecoming—they are all reusable operators. Read them as a child and they are events. Read them as a parent and they become lessons about trust, incentives, boundaries, identity, danger, patience, temptation, loyalty, consequence, and recovery.

Your knife-video example illustrates something adjacent: default-role capture. Most people implicitly accept the role offered by the framing. “What would you do?” plus a visible threat tends to place the viewer in the threatened-person slot. Change the assumed role and a completely different action space becomes salient. You can do this safely in analysis without practicing harm: victim, bystander, medic, negotiator, trainer, prosecutor, designer of the environment, camera operator—each frame exposes different variables.

So the operator is roughly:

same scene + different assumed role -> different salience -> different affordances -> different policy

And your warning about spillover is important. Repeatedly inhabiting a frame can alter what becomes automatically salient later. That is useful when training empathy, leadership, negotiation, or emergency response, but it can also distort judgment if the frame starts leaking into contexts where it no longer fits.

That suggests gated mode switching rather than permanent identification:

enter mode -> use mode-specific priors/tools -> accomplish task -> exit mode -> rebaseline

Dormancy fits neatly into that. A dormant capability has not disappeared; its activation threshold is simply high. Context, incentive, stress, permission, or necessity can lower that threshold and bring the capability back online.

So the deeper pattern is not “be one thing.”

It is maintain a repertoire of possible modes, know which environment each fits, and preserve the ability to switch without letting any one mode colonize the whole system.